course: dictionary
lesson: 979
title: Words for places
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Some words name a place, and the words around them tell you what that place is like. «Bogs and swamps can be dangerous to walk on, so always stay on paths and boardwalks.» Bogs and swamps are wet, muddy ground, and the sentence says to stay on the paths and boardwalks, so a boardwalk must be a safe way across wet ground.
explain: Check your meaning by swapping it into the sentence. «Always stay on paths made of wooden boards» still makes sense, so you have it right. The word is built from two parts, board and walk: a walk made of boards.
explain: In America the word can mean something different. A boardwalk can be a wooden walkway along a beach, with shops, food stands and rides. People say «along the boardwalk» or «on the boardwalk» when they mean that seaside path. Clues such as shops and rides show you which meaning the writer is using.
fact: A boardwalk is a path made of wooden boards.
fact: Boardwalks are often built over sand, mud or wet ground.
fact: In wetlands, a boardwalk protects plants and keeps your feet dry.
fact: In the US, a boardwalk can be a wooden walkway along a beach, with shops, food stands and rides.
fact: The clues around the word, such as «swamps» or «shops», show you which meaning is being used.
exercise: «Bogs and swamps can be dangerous to walk on, so always stay on paths and boardwalks.» What is a boardwalk? | *A path made of wooden boards over wet ground | A tall tree growing in a swamp | A deep pond full of fish | A boat for crossing bogs
exercise: Ruby says, «We walked along the boardwalk and ate ice cream by the shops.» Where were they? | On a rocky mountain top | Deep in a forest with no paths at all | *On a seaside walkway with shops and food stands | Under water in a swamp
exercise: Why might a wetland have boardwalks? | To carry cars across the bog | To make the swamp deeper for boats | *To protect plants and keep your feet dry | To hide the wildlife from visitors
test: Mia says a boardwalk is a road for cars. What is the right answer? | *No, it is a path for walking, made of wooden boards | Yes, it is a road made of tar for cars | Yes, cars drive along it at the seaside
test: According to the sentence about bogs and swamps, what is the safest thing to do? | *Stay on the paths and boardwalks | Walk across the wet ground to find a shortcut | Stand at the water's edge to look for alligators | Swim across the swamp
exam: Which clue tells you a boardwalk is in a wetland, not on a beach? | It has shops along one side | *The text mentions bogs and swamps | It has food stands and rides | It runs along a busy seaside town
